{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_location",
      "description": "The world xy location of the demonstrated block at the END of the demonstration, stored as [x,y]. Identify the block actually lifted, then track its physical identity continuously through all subsequent exchanges of positions; do not retain its original slot. At the video cycle write its final location. During execution retain this location unless the same block is visibly released at a displaced location, in which case update to its new resting xy. This identifies the rightmost red block in episode 70, the leftmost green block in 76 and 56, the leftmost red block in 6, and the demonstrated foreground red block in 95. Infer coordinates using calibration and the block's tabletop geometry, not image left/right as world axes. e.g. [0.014, 0.160]",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate",
      "tolerance": 0
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_repetitions",
      "description": "Number of execution pick-up-and-put-down repetitions completed, excluding the demonstration. Initialize to zero at the video cycle. Increment at the first act cycle showing the lifted target returned to the table and released with open fingers. Thus the release cycles, not the outgoing release commands or subsequent empty-hand retreats, change this value. Retain it on all other cycles, including zero-step cycles and button actions. e.g. 1",
      "value_type": "count",
      "tolerance": 0
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_location",
      "description": "At the video cycle identify the block that the hand lifts. Follow that physical block through the remaining video, including every exchange with an indistinguishable block, and write its final world xy as [x,y]. Use calibrated tabletop geometry to localize it. Never select simply the slot originally grasped: exchanges can change which slot contains the target. During execution carry this location forward; when the observed motion shows the target released at a displaced resting location, update its xy using the visible block and hand geometry. While it is held or occluded retain the previous resting location. e.g. [0.014, 0.160]"
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_repetitions",
      "description": "Initialize to zero in the video cycle. Increment once when this cycle's motion shows the held, raised target descending to the tabletop and the current fingers open to release it. Do not increment merely for closing, lifting, choosing a release waypoint, or lifting the empty hand away. Preserve the count during other motions, button operations, and explicitly zero-step cycles. The demonstration never counts. e.g. 1"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At the video cycle output memory only. Read the required execution repetition count N from the goal: 'again' means one, 'two times' means two, and 'three times' means three. Use target_location to identify the correct physical block in the execution scene, not the block's original demonstration slot. Apply memory updates from observed events before deciding whether more repetitions are needed.\n\nLocalize tabletop objects with the calibrated front and wrist cameras. Estimate a cube's center and edge direction in world coordinates using its visible top surface at approximately z=0.04 m; grasp TCP height is 0.02 m. Use a downward hand, roll pi (equivalently -pi), pitch zero, with yaw aligned to the cube edges modulo pi/2. Do not hard-code an episode's xy or yaw. Heights below are world metres.\n\nWhen execution starts with the hand high and open, move above the target center at z=0.15, keeping it open. If fewer than N repetitions are complete and the open hand has just approached or realigned above the target, descend to its center at z=0.02 and close. The actual reported approach pose can differ from the intended center by about a centimetre; use visual localization rather than treating that error as a new task stage.\n\nIf the hand is low and closed around the target, lift to z=0.15 with the same grasp alignment, keeping it closed. Keep xy nearly fixed, correcting only a few millimetres toward the centered grasp if needed. If the target is held at the raised height, lower at the current TCP xy to z=0.01, preserving orientation, and open at arrival. This is the put-down action, regardless of how many repetitions have already completed.\n\nWhen the hand is open and low after release, retreat vertically to approximately z=0.15, keeping it open and preserving orientation. Do this even after the final repetition; do not move laterally to the button while still low. If the observed motion is this empty-hand retreat and completed_repetitions is less than N, first issue an open-hand realignment above the newly localized target center at z=0.15 with its current edge-aligned yaw. On the cycle after that realignment, descend and close for the next repetition. Distinguish retreat from realignment using this cycle's motion frames, not an assumed unseen action history.\n\nIf completed_repetitions equals N and the released target is on the table with the open hand raised clear, move above the circular stop button at z=0.15, roll=-pi, pitch=0, yaw=0, keeping the gripper open. Localize the button's center, not the center of a neighboring cube. When above the button with open fingers, close the empty gripper while commanding the centered z=0.15 pose. Ensure at least 2 mm displacement from the reported TCP; if necessary use a 5 mm upward offset. When above the button with closed fingers, descend to the button center at commanded z=0.0075, keeping closed. Contact may stop the actual TCP around z=0.0245; this is not a reason to repeatedly push deeper. When the current motion shows that downward button contact and the closed hand is low over the button, retract to z=0.15, still closed.\n\nFor an unanticipated misalignment or failed grasp, use visible geometry to recover: raise clear with an empty open hand, recenter over the remembered target, and retry without increasing the repetition count. Never substitute another block or count a failed lift. Avoid identical-pose commands, unsafe lateral movement at tabletop height, and purposeless waits. If localization is temporarily obscured, retreat vertically to reveal the target rather than guessing a new identity."
}
